    "description": "Department: SRH Reports to: Research and Learning Advisor Based in: Multiple Woredas across Afar and Somali regions. Duration of contract: Based on Ad-Hoc assignments Application Deadline: 5 days after vacancy announcements Who are we? We’re Population Services International (PSI), the world’s leading non‐profit social marketing organization. We work to make it easier for people in the developing world to lead healthier lives and plan the families they desire by marketing affordable products and services that range from mosquito nets to contraceptives to HIV testing. We are a $560m enterprise based in Washington, DC, operating in the private and public sectors in more than 65 countries. Join us! There are over 9,000 “PSI’ers” around the world. It’s a diverse group of entrepreneurs and professionals with an unusually wide range of backgrounds - from the medical industry to the music business - all with unique skills we bring to the job. PSI/Ethiopia wants to reimagine healthcare, put the consumer at the center, and whenever possible, bring quality care to the front door. We achieve health impact in Ethiopia by working with both government and private partners and harvesting technology innovation to bring quality care closer to communities, schools, and homes. We empower consumers by including them in the design of healthcare solutions, offering them more healthcare choices, and helping them to have a voice in influencing national priorities. We work with all market actors in the health system to understand their needs, designing and delivering solutions so that market systems can sustainably serve consumers for the long term, helping to support the Government of Ethiopia in achieving Universal Health Coverage. PSI Ethiopia, in partnership with JSI and the Ministry of Health (MOH), is preparing to implement a three-year project to strengthen RMNCHN and malaria service delivery through enhanced com-munity engagement using the Village Health Leader (VHL) model. The RMNCHN + Malaria Project aim to strengthen the continuum of care and accelerate improvements in maternal, newborn, and child health outcomes across target regions and woredas. The project will be implemented in Nine regions: Afar, Oromia, Amhara, South Ethiopia, Tigray, Sidama, Central Ethiopia, South-west Ethiopia and Somali covering a total of 100 woredas. We are looking for We are seeking highly skilled Qualitative interviewers ( 1 in Afar and 1 in Somali ) and analysts to conduct qualitative interviews and collaborate in qualitative data analysis with the research team for a national community RMNCH, nutrition and Malaria program baseline assessment. Job Responsibilities Qualitative Data Collection : Independently organize and facilitate specialized fieldwork sessions within assigned Woredas, including: In-Depth Interviews (IDIs) with Married Women, Health Extension Workers (HEWs), and Village Health Leaders (VHLs). Key Informant Interviews (KIIs) with local stakeholders. Focus Group Discussions (FGDs) with Young Mothers, Older Mothers, and Male Caretakers. Audio Recording & Notetaking : Ensure high-quality audio recording of all sessions while maintaining precise, contextual field notes ensuring data quality and ethical standards Transcription & Translation : Transcribe audio recordings verbatim from local languages and accurately translate them into English templates. Qualitative Data Analysis : Conduct rigorous thematic data analysis of transcribed text using qualitative software (e.g., Dedoose, NVivo, ATLAS.ti) or structured manual framework matrices and submit all the datasets and field notes to their supervisors. Reporting: Draft and generate clean, thematic summary briefs for each assigned Woreda outlining core cultural barriers, healthcare perceptions, and local socio-ecological factors",
