    "description": "Job Title: WASH Officer Job by: The Well in Action Category: WASH/Engineering Reports to: WASH coordinator and Cluster Coordinator Location: Enda Mehoni, Tigray, Ethiopia. Employment Type: Full Time Duration: Six Month (Possibility of Extension) Salary: Negotiable, Number of Positions : One Application Deadline: Seven days from the date of posting ABOUT THE ORGANIZATION The Well in Action (TWA) is an Ethiopian Civil Society Organization. TWA intervention in Ethiopia is to contribute to the sustainable improvement of the living conditions of the smallholder farmers and deprived people. TWA's core areas of intervention include sustainable livelihoods and food security, rural water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH) emergency response and resilience building, agricultural job creation and youth employment, and education. The organization primarily serves vulnerable rural and urban communities, with a particular focus on poor households, farmers, women, and youth This can be achieved by performing projects which focus on improving agricultural productivity, and stimulating entrepreneurship that are implemented both in rural and urban contexts. Position Overview The WASH officer will provide technical and operational support for the effective delivery of the emergency and recovery WASH interventions in Enda Mehoni, Southern Tigray Zone. Working under the supervision of WASH Coordinator/Cluster coordinator, the post holder will oversee field level day to day implementation and ensure that water supply, water point rehabilitation, sanitation, and hygiene activities are completed on time, within budget, and according to approved technical specification. The WASH Officer will work closely with local government offices, community representatives, contractors, suppliers, WASH committees and other relevant stakeholders to identify WASH needs, plan intervention, monitor work quality, and address implantation challenges. The Officer will also facilitate community participation, promote hygiene awareness, strength local ownership and support the sustainable operation and maintenance of rehabilitated water facilities. 2.1 Purposes of the Position To plan, implement, supervise, monitor, and report on emergency and recovery WASH activities, with particular emphasis on water-supply rehabilitation, sanitation, hygiene promotion, and community participation. Key Duties and Responsibilities Conduct field assessments of existing water-supply schemes and identify rehabilitation, repair, and maintenance needs. Support the preparation of technical designs, Bills of Quantities (BoQs), cost estimates, work plans, and technical specifications for water points re-habitation and related WASH infrastructure. Supervise rehabilitation and construction works, including water points, pipelines, hand pumps, water storage facilities, and institutional or community sanitation facilities, as applicable. Monitor contractors, artisans, and community workers to ensure that works meet approved designs, specifications, quality requirements, and agreed timelines. Monitor construction materials and workmanship, identify technical defects, and recommend corrective actions. Coordinate water-quality testing, source disinfection, and other appropriate measures to help ensure safe water supply. Organize and support hygiene-promotion and behavior-change activities on safe water handling, household water treatment, sanitation, and disease prevention. Support the distribution and proper use of WASH supplies, including water-treatment materials, jerrycans, and dignity or menstrual-hygiene kits, according to project plans. Establish, train, and provide follow-up support to WASH committees and water-user groups to strengthen community ownership and sustainability. Promote participation of women, children, older people, people with disabilities, and other vulnerable groups in WASH activities. Coordinate closely with the Woreda Water, Energy, Health, and other relevant government offices, community leaders, and local stakeholders. Participate in WASH coordination meetings and share relevant field updates with TWA and coordination partners. Support the integration of gender equality, safeguarding, disability inclusion, accountability to affected people, and Protection from Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (PSEA) into project activities. Collect and verify field data, monitor activity progress against approved plans and indicators, and maintain accurate project records. Prepare timely weekly, monthly, and other required progress reports, including updates on rehabilitation works, outputs, challenges, and corrective actions. Document beneficiary feedback, lessons learned, site photographs, work progress, and completion information as required. Report implementation risks, delays, quality concerns, and safeguarding issues promptly through established TWA procedures. Comply with TWA policies, donor requirements, relevant technical standards, and health and safety procedures. N.B.: - TWA requires that all employees take seriously their ethical responsibilities to safeguard our intended program participant, their communities, and all those with whom we work. Staff at all levels have responsibilities to support and develop systems that create and maintain an environment that prevents harassment, sexual exploitation, and abuse, safeguards the rights of program participants and community members (especially women, children’s and people with disability).",
